DATA QA PYTHON-TOOLKIT
=======================

What the Toolkit Can Do
-----------------------
• Provides a quick overall understanding of any tabular dataset.
• Displays dataset summary (file size, rows, columns, and metadata).
• Identifies missing values, non-null values, and completely empty columns.
• Shows unique count, duplicate count, and uniqueness status for every column.
• Displays the most frequent (Top N) values to understand data distribution.
• Detects leading and trailing whitespace issues.
• Generates string length statistics to identify inconsistent text values.
• Produces numeric summaries (min, max, mean, standard deviation) and detects outliers.
• Automatically identifies date columns and reports their date ranges.
• Standardizes common missing value representations (NULL, NaN, N/A, etc.).
• Generates detailed TXT and color-coded Excel QA reports.
• Works with multiple file formats (CSV, TSV, Excel, JSON, XML, Parquet).
• Can be executed from the command line or imported as a reusable Python module.
• Helps analysts quickly assess data quality before performing further analysis or validation.

Pros
----
• Provides a comprehensive overview of the dataset in a single run.
• Reduces manual effort and saves significant QA time.
• Helps identify common data quality issues at an early stage.
• Easy to use, reusable, and fully automated.
• Modular design makes it easy to extend with additional QA checks.
• Suitable for exploratory data analysis and initial data validation.
• Generates well-structured reports for documentation and review.
• Supports multiple data sources without requiring code changes.

Cons
----
• Focuses on generic data profiling rather than business-specific validation.
• Does not validate schema, data types, or business rules.
• Does not perform cross-column or cross-file consistency checks.
• No regex-based validation for fields such as email, phone number, or URL.
• No row-level/composite duplicate detection.
• No data quality score or visual dashboards.
• Loads the complete dataset into memory, which may impact performance on very large files.